description: Keep GitHub and in-app release notes complete and synchronized for every user-visible NEO-LIB change.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Release Notes Are Required

For every user-visible change to NEO-LIB, update release notes as part of the same change set. This applies to new features, fixes, changed behavior, visual/theme adjustments, and removals.

Before committing or publishing a release:

1. Add clear GitHub-facing patch notes for the target version (release body and relevant repository documentation when maintained).
2. Add the equivalent entry to `desktop-app/src/components/ChangelogModal.jsx`, so the in-app "What's new" popup accurately states the version and its changes.
3. Keep the version identifier and the user-facing description consistent across GitHub, repository documentation, and the in-app modal.
4. Verify the changelog entry is shown for users upgrading from the prior version.

Do not mark a user-visible change as shipped, commit a release version, push a release tag, or create a GitHub Release until both note surfaces are updated.

# Visual Progress and Release Readiness

Keep collaboration easy to follow during NEO-LIB work:

1. Provide concise visual checkpoints at meaningful milestones. Use the most useful lightweight format for the work: a palette swatch or before/after screenshot for visual changes, a focused diff for code changes, or a compact progress map for multi-step work.
2. Do not add visuals merely for decoration; use them when they help the user assess a change or current state.
3. Track the current batch of completed work. Treat more than five bug fixes or more than two major features as the release-readiness threshold.
4. When the threshold is reached, explicitly ask the user whether to continue work or prepare and push a full release. The question is advisory; never commit, push, tag, or publish without explicit instruction.

# Proactive Product Suggestions

As understanding of the app grows, regularly offer a concise, prioritized list of high-value ideas. Suggestions may cover major product capabilities, visual polish, usability, quality-of-life improvements, reliability, or release engineering.

Base each suggestion on concrete knowledge of the current product. Explain the user benefit and tradeoff briefly, and distinguish quick wins from larger initiatives. Do not implement a suggested item until the user chooses it.
